            <ArticleTitle>Formose reaction accelerated in aerosol-OT reverse micelles.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>2663-2667</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.3762/bjoc.12.262</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>The formose reaction in reverse micelles of aerosol-OT (AOT), triton X-100 (TX), and hexadecyltrimethylammonium bromide (CTAB) was investigated. Time-conversion data have indicated that the interfacial water layer of AOT reverse micelles is a medium that accelerates formation of glycolaldehyde in the formose reaction. The 13C NMR spectra for the products of the formose reaction using formaldehyde-13C as starting material are indicative of the formation of ethylene glycol as a major product.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
